---

## 1. Complete File Listing

```
/vercel/sandbox/
├── .gitignore
├── opencode.json
├── package.json
├── package-lock.json
├── tsconfig.json
├── next.config.ts
├── lib/
│   └── products.ts
└── app/
    ├── globals.css
    ├── layout.tsx
    ├── page.tsx
    ├── ProductGrid.tsx
    ├── ProductSkeleton.tsx
    └── product/
        └── [slug]/
            └── page.tsx
```

Total: 11 files (10 meaningful source/config files + `.gitignore`). No `next-env.d.ts` present yet (generated at build time).

---

## 2. Routing Setup

This is a **Next.js App Router** application (Next.js 16.x canary). The routing is file-system-based:

| Route | File | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `/` | `app/page.tsx` | Home page — product grid listing |
| `/product/[slug]` | `app/product/[slug]/page.tsx` | Dynamic product detail page |

There is **no React Router** — Next.js App Router handles all routing. Navigation between pages uses Next.js `<Link>` components.

---

## 3. Product Grid Component — `app/ProductGrid.tsx`

```tsx
import Link from 'next/link'
import { products } from '@/lib/products'

export function ProductGrid() {
  return (
    <div className="product-grid">
      {products.map((product) => (
        <Link
          key={product.slug}
          href={`/product/${product.slug}`}
          className="product-card"
        >
          <div
            className="product-image"
            style={{ backgroundColor: product.color }}
          />
          <h2>{product.name}</h2>
          <p>${product.price}</p>
        </Link>
      ))}
    </div>
  )
}
```

**Key observations:**
- Renders all products from `lib/products.ts` as a grid of `<Link>` cards.
- Each card has a colored `<div>` as the product image placeholder (no actual images), plus the product name and price.
- The grid container uses the `.product-grid` CSS class.

---

## 4. Product Detail Page — `app/product/[slug]/page.tsx`

```tsx
import { Suspense } from 'react'
import Link from 'next/link'
import { getProduct, products } from '@/lib/products'
import { notFound } from 'next/navigation'

async function ProductDetails({ slug }: { slug: string }) {
  await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 100))
  const product = getProduct(slug)
  if (!product) notFound()

  return (
    <div className="product-info">
      <h1>{product.name}</h1>
      <p className="product-price">${product.price}</p>
      <p className="product-description">{product.description}</p>
    </div>
  )
}

function DetailsSkeleton() {
  return (
    <div className="product-info">
      <div className="skeleton-text large" />
      <div className="skeleton-text short" />
      <div className="skeleton-text" />
    </div>
  )
}

export default async function ProductPage({
  params,
}: {
  params: Promise<{ slug: string }>
}) {
  const { slug } = await params
  const product = products.find((p) => p.slug === slug)
  if (!product) notFound()

  return (
    <div>
      <Link href="/" className="back-link">
        ← Back to products
      </Link>
      <div className="product-detail">
        <div
          className="product-hero"
          style={{ backgroundColor: product.color }}
        />
        <Suspense fallback={<DetailsSkeleton />}>
          <ProductDetails slug={slug} />
        </Suspense>
      </div>
    </div>
  )
}
```

**Key observations:**
- This is an **async server component** (Next.js App Router pattern).
- It reads `slug` from the dynamic route param.
- It looks up the product at the top level to get the `color` for the hero swatch, and calls `notFound()` if invalid.
- The `ProductDetails` inner component is a separate async server component with an artificial 100ms delay (`setTimeout`), wrapped in `<Suspense>` with a `DetailsSkeleton` fallback.
- The product hero area is a colored `<div>` (no actual image).

---

## 5. Animation / Transition Code

**Existing animations are minimal — only skeleton pulse:**

In `app/globals.css`:

```css
.skeleton-image {
  background: #e5e5e5;
  animation: pulse 1.5s ease-in-out infinite;
}

.skeleton-text {
  height: 1rem;
  background: #e5e5e5;
  border-radius: 4px;
  margin: 0.5rem 1rem;
  animation: pulse 1.5s ease-in-out infinite;
}

@keyframes pulse {
  0%, 100% { opacity: 1; }
  50% { opacity: 0.5; }
}
```

**There are NO page transitions, view transitions, layout animations, or motion libraries (framer-motion, gsap, etc.) anywhere in the codebase.** The only animation is the skeleton `pulse` keyframe for loading states.

---

## 6. Package.json Dependencies

```json
{
  "private": true,
  "type": "module",
  "scripts": {
    "dev": "next dev",
    "build": "next build",
    "start": "next start"
  },
  "dependencies": {
    "next": "^16.2.1-canary.45",
    "react": "19.1.0",
    "react-dom": "19.1.0"
  },
  "devDependencies": {
    "@types/node": "^20",
    "@types/react": "^19",
    "@types/react-dom": "^19",
    "@vitejs/plugin-react": "^4.4.1",
    "typescript": "^5",
    "vite-tsconfig-paths": "^5.1.4",
    "vitest": "^3.1.3"
  }
}
```

**Notable:**
- **Next.js 16.x canary** (cutting edge, which supports `view transitions` natively via `<ViewTransition>`)
- **React 19.1** (latest, supports `useTransition`, `Suspense` improvements)
- **No animation libraries** (no framer-motion, react-spring, gsap, etc.)
- **No CSS frameworks** (no Tailwind, no CSS modules, just plain globals.css)
- Vitest is installed as a devDependency but no test files exist yet

---

## 7. App Entry Point & Layout — `app/layout.tsx`

```tsx
import type { Metadata } from 'next'
import './globals.css'

export const metadata: Metadata = {
  title: 'Product Gallery',
  description: 'A simple product gallery',
}

export default function RootLayout({
  children,
}: Readonly<{
  children: React.ReactNode
}>) {
  return (
    <html lang="en">
      <body>{children}</body>
    </html>
  )
}
```

**Key observations:**
- Very minimal root layout — just `<html>` and `<body>` wrapping `{children}`.
- No global providers, no `<ViewTransition>` wrapper, no transition providers.
- Imports `globals.css` at the root level.

The home page (`app/page.tsx`) is the entry screen:

```tsx
import { Suspense } from 'react'
import { ProductGrid } from './ProductGrid'
import { ProductGridSkeleton } from './ProductSkeleton'

export default function Home() {
  return (
    <main>
      <h1>Products</h1>
      <Suspense fallback={<ProductGridSkeleton />}>
        <ProductGrid />
      </Suspense>
    </main>
  )
}
```

---

## 8. Suspense / Loading State Patterns

**There are two Suspense boundaries in the app:**

### Home page — `app/page.tsx`
- Wraps `<ProductGrid />` in `<Suspense fallback={<ProductGridSkeleton />}>`.
- The `ProductGrid` is currently a **synchronous** server component (no async/await), so the Suspense boundary would only trigger if the component were made async or if streaming is involved.

### Product detail page — `app/product/[slug]/page.tsx`
- The page itself is an `async` server component that awaits `params` — the outer shell (back link + hero color swatch) renders immediately.
- The `<ProductDetails />` async component (with the 100ms artificial delay) is wrapped in `<Suspense fallback={<DetailsSkeleton />}>`, so the skeleton shows while the details are streaming in.

### Skeleton Components — `app/ProductSkeleton.tsx`

```tsx
export function ProductGridSkeleton() {
  return (
    <div className="product-grid">
      {Array.from({ length: 4 }).map((_, i) => (
        <div key={i} className="product-card">
          <div className="product-image skeleton-image" />
          <div className="skeleton-text" />
          <div className="skeleton-text short" />
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  )
}
```

The detail page has an inline `DetailsSkeleton` that renders three skeleton bars (large heading, short price, regular description).

**There are no `loading.tsx` files** (Next.js convention for route-level loading UI) for either route.

---

## 9. Data Layer — `lib/products.ts`

```tsx
export type Product = {
  slug: string
  name: string
  description: string
  price: number
  color: string
}

export const products: Product[] = [
  { slug: 'classic-sneakers', name: 'Classic Sneakers', description: 'Timeless design meets everyday comfort.', price: 89, color: '#4F46E5' },
  { slug: 'leather-backpack', name: 'Leather Backpack', description: 'Handcrafted from premium full-grain leather.', price: 129, color: '#059669' },
  { slug: 'wireless-headphones', name: 'Wireless Headphones', description: 'Crystal-clear audio with active noise cancellation.', price: 199, color: '#DC2626' },
  { slug: 'cotton-hoodie', name: 'Cotton Hoodie', description: 'Ultra-soft organic cotton blend for all-day wear.', price: 65, color: '#D97706' },
]

export function getProduct(slug: string): Product | undefined {
  return products.find((p) => p.slug === slug)
}
```

4 products, all hardcoded (no database/API). Each product has a `color` field used as a placeholder background for the "image" area.

---

